<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khalchayan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1st century BCE–1st century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Khalchayan is an archaeological site in the Surkhandarya region of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>, excavated by Galina Pugachenkova from 1959. A hall or palace there was decorated with painted clay sculptures of nomad rulers, warriors and deities in Hellenistic style, dated to around the 1st century BCE, which are among the earliest images of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a> and Kushan elite.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khalchayan.jpg" alt="Khalchayan" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ALFGRN · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kushan_prince_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their early coinage imitated Greco-Bactrian issues, and at <b>Khalchayan</b> in southern Uzbekistan a palace or hall was decorated with painted clay sculptures of nomad aristocrats in Hellenistic style.</blockquote><a href="/empire/yuezhi">Yuezhi · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1986 the art historian German Hafner first suggested a Hellenistic link, and Duan Qingbo, the site’s chief archaeologist from 1998 to 2006, saw a close parallel only in the later <b>Khalchayan</b> statuary of Central Asia.</blockquote><a href="/work/terracotta-army">Terracotta Army · Reception</a></li></ul></section>
